Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: tcpm: fail a dr_set that changed no role

tcpm_dr_set() waits for the swap to complete and returns the status it
was completed with.  A refusal and the timeouts complete it with an
error.  Whatever else ends a swap leaves it pending, and the next
SRC_READY or SNK_READY completes it with status 0, also when the data
role is still the old one.  A write to the port's data_role then
succeeds without having done anything.

On the SM-X910 a Soft Reset cut a DR_Swap short.  The contract was back
157 ms later, SNK_READY completed the swap, and the write of "host"
returned 0 with the port still a device.

Look at the data role once the swap has completed with status 0, and
return -EAGAIN when it is not the one that was asked for.  That also
covers a partner without PD, where the swap is a port reset that can
bring the old roles back.

tcpm_pr_set() and tcpm_vconn_set() complete the same way and are left as
they are.
---
 drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c
@@ -7355,6 +7355,21 @@ static int tcpm_dr_set(struct typec_port
 	else
 		ret = port->swap_status;
 
+	/*
+	 * The next ready state completes the swap with status 0 whatever
+	 * became of it, also after a Soft Reset that cut it short and after
+	 * a port reset that brought the old roles back.  Only the data role
+	 * says whether it happened.
+	 */
+	if (!ret) {
+		mutex_lock(&port->lock);
+		if (port->data_role != data) {
+			tcpm_log(port, "Data role swap ended with the role unchanged");
+			ret = -EAGAIN;
+		}
+		mutex_unlock(&port->lock);
+	}
+
 	port->non_pd_role_swap = false;
 	goto swap_unlock;
 
